<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Smendes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1077–1052 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Smendes was a ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanis">Tanis</a> at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>, who founded the Twenty-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Dynasty</a>. By around 1100 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> was governed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/high-priest-of-amun">High Priest of Amun</a> while Smendes controlled Lower Egypt. This division marked the end of the New Kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/smendes.jpg" alt="Smendes" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canopic_Smendes_Met.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Smendes may have been a son of Hrere, Chief of the Harem of Amun-Re and probably the wife of a high priest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>. If so, he was the brother of Nodjmet and the brother-in-law of the High Priests Herihor and Piankh. He married Tentamun, likely a daughter of Ramesses IX, and he is described as son-in-law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-xi">Ramesses XI</a>. His birth name was Nesbanebdjed, meaning He of the Ram, Lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mendes">Mendes</a>; Smendes is the Greek form used by later writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> and Sextus Africanus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Smendes succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-xi">Ramesses XI</a>, the last ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twentieth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twentieth Dynasty</a>, and founded the Twenty-First. He may have resided at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanis">Tanis</a> or at Memphis, and blocks and statues at Tanis bear his names, some of them reused from earlier Ramesside kings. Tanis became the capital under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psusennes-i">Psusennes I</a>, but the move from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pi-ramesses">Pi-Ramesses</a> may have begun with Smendes.</p><p>He ruled a divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. His nominal authority in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> is attested by a quarry stela at Ed-Dibabiya, which tells how he, residing in Memphis, ordered repairs to the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxor">Luxor</a> after a flood threat, and by a graffito at the Temple of Monthu at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a>. Middle and Upper Egypt were effectively under the High Priests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pinedjem-i">Pinedjem I</a>, Masaharta and Menkheperre.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manetho">Manetho</a>'s Epitome assigns him twenty-six years. A date in Year 25 on the Banishment Stela, recording how Menkheperre put down a revolt at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> and exiled its leaders to the oases of the Western Desert, can refer only to Smendes. His throne name, Hedjkheperre Setepenre, was later adopted by five kings, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shoshenq-i">Shoshenq I</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>He appears in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/story-of-wenamun">Report of Wenamun</a>, a story in which a priest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herihor">Herihor</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> first calls on Smendes at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanis">Tanis</a> and receives a ship from him. Egyptologists question its historical accuracy, and since Karl Jansen-Winkeln proposed reversing the order of Herihor and Piankh, Arno Egberts has argued that the tale is set in Smendes's fifth year. He was succeeded by Amenemnisu, who may have been his son. His tomb has not been found; a canopic jar of his is in the Metropolitan Museum of Art.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Smendes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By approximately 1080 BCE, the High Priest of Amun Herihor governed Upper Egypt as a de facto independent ruler, while <b>Smendes</b> controlled Lower Egypt from Tanis.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>On the death of Ramesses XI around 1077 BCE, <b>Smendes</b>, who had governed the north from the Delta, became king and founded the Twenty-first Dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/twenty-first-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-first Dynasty of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
